Hello,

I've just prepared the first release of a tool for fast & accurate
simulation of multiprocessors that runs on the PC. It may be of interest
primarily to the people who do architecture studies, but it can also
be employed in parallel algorithms evaluation. The simulator is,
as it is usually called, "execution-driven".

Existing tools for this purpose, such as TangoLite from Stanford,
work (to my knowledge) only on MIPS machines. They are by their nature
non-portable, since they are bound to the instruction set of the host
computer. This one is too, and it works only on i486/P5/P6 PC,
on Linux.

If you are interested, check out
http://galeb.etf.bg.ac.yu/~dav0r/limes/index.html.

There is a little more information there, and you can download the
complete documentation (.doc or .ps), and the tool itself, of course.
The sources are in the archive, and it's all free (copyright (c) 1996
Davor Magdic, no warranty, see the GPL, etc.).

For any questions/comments, please mail me at
magdic00390d@buef31.etf.bg.ac.yu.

Greetings,
Davor

