Report on the Linux installfest in Cologne
- ------------------------------------------

For the first time in their 2-years tradition the tutorials on Linux
at the universtity of cologne were accompanied by an "installfest".

30 newbies were happy to bring their PC along and got assistance in
installing Linux from 30 experts. A big "thank you" to everyone who
supported the event namely the people that are listed on the end of
this e-mail.

Both, the tutorials and the "installfest" were a great success and all
people went home satisfied afterwards. Even those who had driven several
100 kilometers (which is _much_ for German circumstances).

I write this report to encourage other Linux-enthusiasts to launch similar
events.


The tutorials
- -------------

The computer centre department of the university of cologne  ("Regionales
Rechenzentrum der Universitaet zu Koeln") offers 4-day tutorials on Linux
twice a year (april/october). Of course the tutorials are free of charge
for everyone and announcements are made in every related Linux-newsgroup.

My experience as an referent for these tutorials shows that the following
issues are of high interest for newbies:
        
   1st day:

      - what is Linux (GNU-idea|distribution|operating system|kernel)
      - applications for Linux
      - documentation in elctronic and printed form

      - the basic concepts:
              * multi-user, multi-tasking
              * everything is seen as file (e.g. "cat /dev/mouse")
              * file-permissions
              * layout of the directory tree (overview only, the
                details are encountered in the following tutorials)

      - sample installation by hand (_no_ menu at all)


   2nd day:

      - login and logout
      - virtual consoles and their capabilities

      - the first steps with Linux (basic shell commands)
      - file-permissions (once again)
      - alternatives to the shell

      - a (highly insecure) cgi-script as a sample application
        for shell-scripts
      - "dialog" for nicer shell-programming


   3rd day:

      - inspecting the boot-procedure of Linux

      - configuring of a Linux-system (environment variables, global
        and individual (=dot) config-files, various file-formats,
        shell-Skripts, X-resoures)
      - maintaining different users (passwd-database, group-database)

      - installation of software that is not supplied by a distribution
        (what are .h-files, what are libraries, why "configure" and
        "make"; sample installation of the midnight commander)

      - building and installing a hand-crafted Linux-kernel
        (especially the ability to boot it straight from a floppy)


  4th day:

      - internet
      - X11


I experimented with packing the stuff of 4 days into a 1-day tutorial in
April and had 90 attendees: even employed persons are willing to take a
1-day holiday to learn about Linux.
But this form of the course (resulting in 9 hours of lectures) was far to
straining for both, the attendees and me so I switched back to the old
scheme this october.


The installfest
- ---------------

To enhance the chance of newbies to get a working Linux system the cologne
Linux user group aka "Linux Workshop Koeln" organised an installfest.
[The homepage of our usergroup is http://www.uni-koeln.de/themen/linux/
 ... written in German but the logo is international.] :-)

The first thing we did was asking around in the net for experts which
should guide the newbies through the installation of Linux.
48 hours afterwards 25 experts had signed up and we announced the event.
Based on these numbers we expected a huge interest and therefore we
limited the number of attendees to 50 computers (not enough desks).

Surprise surprise, even after 1 week only 3 newbies did respond to our
invitation. It seems that the net is the wrong place to invite
Linux-newbies because everyone who struggles with hooking up to the
internet automatically becomes an expert. ;-)


Well, Jens combined some pinguin-photos he took at the cologne zoo into a
fantastic poster and we hung up copies in bookstores and around the
university.
"Vobis" was the only computer-shop that was interested in our poster,
all others were sorry but "had no permission". Poor.

Of course our friends from the bookstore "J.F. Lehmanns" wanted 2 posters
and the bookstores "VUB", "Gonski" and "Mayersche" (Neumarkt) were all
very happy to get a copy, too. The poster is available from

      http://www.uni-koeln.de/themen/linux/poster.tar.gz

Attention, it's _huge_. Even on very fast machines "ghostscript" will take
several minutes to display it. :-(
Our public relations worked and after some days we had 20 subscriptions
for the installfest.


To have a sufficient large room we rent a tent that usually serves for
dinner and lunch of the students. It was the only "room" for which we
did not need thousand of keys and permissions to walk unattended through 
buildings on a public holiday.
Of course we wanted to do it on weekends or holidays to give everyone a
chance to participate.

Another reason for this tent was that it could be perfectly reached by
car and enough parking places were outside.
The drawbacks of it were some minor problems with the electrical power
that were solved 30 minutes after the beginning and the missing toilets.

Because the whole event was non-commercial and a service free of charge
to the students, we only had to pay for the running costs of the tent
which were really low.


Preparations started on October the 3rd at 10am which was a little bit too
late to get everything plugged in in time. The first impatient newbies came
in at 10:45 and had to wait for first help until 11:15.
Beside that every newbie praised the immediate help she/he received from
all those experts. Hm, nothing new in the Linux-community. :-)

Our computer service department lent us some network-equipment so we were
able to build an intranet (domain "party.net") consisting of a nameserver,
a newsserver (DEC Alpha running Linux), a ftp/NFS-Server and every computer
that was equipped with an ethernetcard. 
With this network the people had the choice to install the newest Debian-
or RedHat-Distribution via ftp or to use their own CDs (mostly SuSE).


Considering the fact that there was no coordination in what every expert
had to do the performance of the whole event was astonishing good.


