Hello amateur radio operators (who are programmers) of the world!

More than a year ago, I wrote a very simple program to control
a Yaesu FT-890 in Linux so I could learn C.  The program works, but
has a terrible interface.  I have had many folks contact me about
expanding the program but I never hear from them again.  The
program has been distributed to at least 30 hams worldwide, meaning
there is demand for a good rig control program for Linux.  It is
at the limit of my ability and must be turned over to someone that
can expand it.
So, this leads me to...

I am looking for someone to take over where I left off, to maintain
the program and expand its uses.  Do you want to write and maintain
a nice X interface for transceivers (Yaesus, Icoms, Kenwoods and any
others) that do computer interfacing?  The serial stuff is out of the
way, and most all opcodes are implemented, there just lacks a nice
interface.  It needs to be free to anyone with the source included
and not sellable (keeping with the Linux community's credo: copyleft).
There is a demand for this, and the feature possibilities are endless.
Besides, we need a good rig control program for Linux to go along
with SatTrack, xearth, other Linux packet programs, etc.

If you are interested in being the maintainer of my program and in
writing a graphical front end for transceivers in X (the serial stuff
is done-the hardest part), please contact me so we can arrange who 
will maintain it.  Let's write more ham software!  It's only as good
as we make it.

Thanks, hope to hear from many programming hams!

