Announcing the Linux/m68k for Macintosh Project!

This is a project to get the port of Linux for Motorola 680x0-series
processors working on 68k-based Macintosh computers.  We have just gotten
started, and very little code has been written so far.  Any and all
knowledgeable programmers who wish to contribute to this project are
welcome to join!


MANIFESTO:

Now is an ideal time to begin work on such a project.  Why?

* Linux/m68k development has caught up with the mainstream kernel sources.
  Thanks to the efforts of Jes Degn Soerensen and company, the 2.0.xx releases
  have been stable and very encouraging.

* Apple is releasing MkLinux, an invaluable source of information (and
  possibly source code) useful for programming the Macintosh hardware.
  Much of the device driver portion of the MK microkernel (but not all)
  should be portable back to the later 68k Macintosh series.

* MacBSD now supports the majority of 68k-based desktop Macs, with
  encouraging developments in video and ADB support.  
  (Unfortunately, BSD-copylefted code cannot be included in Linux unless
  the original authors re-license the code to be released under the GPL;
  still, MacBSD's approaches to a number of problems provide good examples
  from which Linux development work can follow.) 

* And of course, someone had to do it sooner or later...

Many 68k-based Macs, with years of dependable service still ahead of them,
are slowly beginning to gather dust and become obsolete as Apple moves
away from the Motorola-based Macintosh platform in favor of the more
advanced PowerPC technology.  With luck and a lot of work, hopefully this
project will give new a usefulness to these machines.  Besides, we just
might make a few PPC owners envious of their 68k brethren for once... :-) 


CURRENT STATUS:

Very little work on this project has been done so far.  At present we (all
four/five of us) have little documentation over and above that freely
available on the Net, and not much access to 68k-based machines suitable
for development work.  Our supply of spare time isn't that great either... 

Any 68k Mac programmer who has experience with Linux, MkLinux, MacBSD, or
Mac hardware internals is welcome to join in!  Additionally, donations of
documentation, technical info, and other expertise are encouraged!
(Especially if you work for Apple!)


FEATURES/PROJECTS:

Here is a sampling of the projects that need to be taken up in order to
get Linux/m68k working on the Mac, in random order - more detailed info
about each of these is available at the web page (see below).  If you're
interested in working on any of these, mail the project coordinator, Rob
Pelkey <rpelkey@bates.edu> - anyone who can contribute in any way is
welcome to do so!

* Startup Code
* Interrupt Handling Code
* SCSI Support
* Serial Support
* IDE Support
* ADB Driver
* Video Support
* Floppy Driver
* Audio Driver
* Ethernet Driver


IMPORTANT DISCLAIMER:

**WE DO NOT HAVE A WORKING KERNEL YET**, and probably will not for some
time.  Therefore we ARE NOT looking for testers at this time.  We will
announce when things begin working, at which point we will need testers.


FOR MORE INFO:

Check out the Linux/m68k for Macintosh Project home page:

	http://www.bates.edu/~rpelkey/linux-mac68k/

Also, a mailing list has been set up for those working on the project;
to subscribe, send mail to majordomo@rpelkey.bates.edu with the line
"subscribe linux-mac68k" in the message body.

Direct any questions about this project to the project coordinator, Rob
Pelkey <rpelkey@bates.edu>, or to the mailing lists.


MISCELLANIA:

Note that this is *not* Linux for Power Macintosh computers - for that,
check out Apple/OSF's MkLinux at <http://www.mklinux.apple.com/>, or
better yet, give the Linux/PPC people <http://www.linuxppc.org/> a hand
porting their work to the PowerMac. 

Also, note that this is completely unrelated to the vaporware "MacLinux"
port by Martin Sjolin two years ago - although we're trying to accomplish
the same thing...  :-)

Please notify me if you re-post this message on your web site, or add
links to the project's mailing list or website on your pages.  This is so
that I know who I should notify if/when any of the above addresses change.
Thanks!


