-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

I have been working on LGC off-and-on the past few months (mostly *on* for the
last month).  Here are some of the new developments:

	* Improved IPX emulation.
	* Ability to join games in progress (some games, like Quake, allow
	  joining games in progress, which is a *very* cool feature, and one
	  which I hope will become standard in networkable games).
	* Improved stability.
	* Got rid of unnecessary "searching for nodes" phase of game setup.
	* Improved ncurses code in server.
	* Batch files (ul.bat and dl.bat) are used for file xfers now, so you
	  can use ZModem implementations besides DSZ with LGC-Comm.
	* Colorized nicknames.
	* LGC-Comm does ANSI emulations now - it no longer depends on ANSI.SYS
	* Virtual networks may now have a maximum of 64 nodes, as opposed to
	  the old limit of 8 nodes (which was just *so* restrictive ;-)
	* Server changes: added/extended commands, improved appearance, better
	  online help.
	* Various internal cleanups.
	* Switched DOS compilers: Borland C/C++ 3.1 -> Watcom C/C++ 10.6
          (WAY bigger version # -> WAY better compiler, right?? ;-)

