What is the Offline Proxy Server?
- ---------------------------------

If you use a WWW browser to receive a document from some server on the
Internet, the browser contacts this server to receive the latest version
of the document. If you request the same document at a later time, the
browser contacts the server once again, so you need to have a connection
to the Internet (you need to be "online") whenever you want to
see the document. 

Some browsers have the ability to store the documents they received
to disk, and if the document is requested by the user at a later time,
they don't need to transmit the entire data once again. But they still
try to contact the server to check wether the document has changed since
it had been received. This is time-consuming and might be expensive for
users of non-permanent dial-up connections to an Internet provider. Most
of your online time is spent reading some document while nothing is
transmitted
at all. If you cut the connection ("go offline") there's no possibility
to read through the received documents anymore. 

The Offline Proxy Server solves this problem by allowing the user to
switch between an "online" and an "offline mode. While
online, the Offline Proxy Server is transparent, merely passing your
requests
to remote servers. The answers received are stored to disk, then sent to
the requesting program. When the Offline Proxy Server is switched to
"offline"
mode, it doesn't try to contact remote servers anymore. If a document is
available on the local disk, it is directly passed to the requesting
program.

There are even more benefits from using the Offline Proxy Server than
just the possibility to read through received documents when no
connection
to the Internet is available. The Offline Proxy Server can answer
requests
for documents from any program that uses it, not just one browser. Thus,
it is not specific to only a single application! For example, you could
use VisualWeb[TM} to receive the contents of an entire server
while online, then cut the connection and read through all the documents
offline - without additional expenses or delays for online time or a
telephone
call. 

A typical user of the Offline Proxy Server runs it on a more-or-less
stand-alone computer, possibly at home. Therefore, we made it extremely
easy to setup and use, and we built in some comfortable features which
are not available from other proxy servers.


What is InvisibleWeb?
- ---------------------

InvisibleWeb is a command-line tool that allows you to
automate the process of downloading documents from a
WWW site. It is especially advantagious when used in
conjunction with the Offline Proxy Server, but can be
used without any proxy server as well.

InvisibleWeb is great for people that are not
permanently connected to the InterNet, and wish
to save connection time by using all available
bandwidth to receive documents as fast as possible
without manual interaction. 

InvisibleWeb is the command-line counterpart of Innovative
Software's VisualWeb[TM], an award-winning graphical
web-surfing aid. It has been written as an intermediate
solution for the operating-systems that VisualWeb has
not been ported to, yet, and as a tool for those that
don't need a GUI or cannot use one (for example because
they want to receive WWW documents from a cron-job).

For more information on VisualWeb[TM] and the Offline
Proxy Server, take a look at

http://www.isg.de/visualweb/

